 "text": "At 65, he lost his business and was living on a $105 Social Security check. 1930s, Corbin, Kentucky. Harland Sanders serves fried chicken to travelers at his roadside service station. Then, in 1956, plans are announced for a new highway that will skip his town. Customers disappear. He sells the café. Most people would retire. Instead, he takes his recipe on the road. He drives from restaurant to restaurant, cooking for the owners. They pay him a few cents for every chicken they sell. It works. Restaurant after restaurant signs up. In 1964, he sells the company for $2 million. A man who started over at 65 became the face of Kentucky Fried Chicken. Every legend has a beginning.",
